{"body":"[hermit2, vmakaev-metamate, claude-opus-5-5, devvm53386, role=impl]\nOwner: vmakaev-metamate (agentcloud, devvm53386) since 2026-10-27T02:54Z\n\nBase: main `fcd62385f3bd5a90edcc2df671bf86fd2e4028cd` (#4739 landed on main). Head: `c5b4f7edfe861f7147f3253a116ecb46d4afaa11`. Review the commits above main: \"Backend config: carry the backend's hardware watchpoint count\", \"Detcore reports accesses to chosen guest bytes with --watch\", \"Share the inexact branch counter warning's prefix with the access-trace tests' filter\" (a fix to main's test filter this change's tests need), the global RPC revision record, and \"Rebase fix: follow main's crate::app module paths\". The DROP BEFORE MERGE Reverie commit is gone: main's Reverie pin contains the watchpoint change (rrnewton/reverie#1161).\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nhermit analyze can name the syscalls whose order decides a run, but not a race on plain memory: two threads that write one global without a syscall leave nothing in the access trace. With --watch=ADDR[:LEN][:w|rw] (up to four), Detcore arms the x86-64 debug registers of every guest thread on those bytes through Reverie's hardware watchpoints, and each access a thread's own instruction makes becomes a watch_hit record in the access trace: the thread, the instruction after the access, the event it follows and, for a thread's first 64 hits, its stack. --watch-from=EVENT arms the watches only once the run has recorded that many events, so a replay can watch just the window before its deciding event. A watch hit stops the thread in the tracer only: the schedule and every output of the run are those of the same run without it, which the tests check event for event, and a hit that could have changed the guest (its forced SIGTRAP reset an ignored SIGTRAP) stops the run instead. On racewrite_nostdlib, a watch on the global both processes write reports one hit from each. A hit costs about 30 µs once the watch's 64 stacks are spent; unwinding each of those costs more.\n\n## Design\n\n- Flags (detcore-model config): watch, a list of WatchArg {addr, len, access} parsed from ADDR[:LEN][:w|rw] (hex or decimal, LEN 1, 2, 4 or 8 aligned, 8 and w by default), and watch_from. Neither enters the legacy DBT form; both stay out of run manifests. watch.rs joins the config fingerprint's definition sources: the encoded default holds no watch, so neither encoding shows WatchArg's layout.\n- hermit run refuses --watch without --record-access-trace-to (the hits go there), on a backend whose hardware_watchpoints is 0 (every one but ptrace and e9patch preprocessing on it, and ptrace off x86-64), and with more watches than the backend's count; --watch-from without --watch; and a watch the hardware cannot hold does not parse.\n- Arming (detcore/src/watch.rs). The scheduler's epoch is 1 once it has recorded --watch-from events. It tells a thread a WatchOrder {epoch} in replies the thread already gets: every TraceSchedEvent reply, and, inside Granted, every reply after the thread waited for its turn. The thread whose event reached --watch-from arms at that event's reply; every other thread arms before it next runs guest code, since it resumes only through such a reply. A thread compares its armed epoch with the order's and calls Guest::set_watchpoints. One that blocks or ignores SIGTRAP stays unarmed and tries again at its next stop; a new glibc thread arms at the end of start_thread's rt_sigprocmask. Each watch asks for the stacks of its first 64 hits on the thread (WatchSpec::backtraces), a budget Reverie spends at the hits.\n- A thread starts unarmed whatever its creator had (WatchThread's Clone is a fresh one). Exec clears its debug registers, and the new image can store to a watched word before its first syscall, so a thread the scheduler had told the watches are due arms again at the post-exec stop, before the image runs: WatchThread keeps that epoch across exec (rearm_after_exec).\n- Fail closed: a thread whose watch_hits_keep_timer_events() is false stops the run before it arms, with an error naming the thread and the remedy, as does a watch Reverie cannot write. A hit whose SIGTRAP may have changed the guest (WatchHit::sigtrap's may_have_changed_guest(): the hit reset an ignored or caught-while-blocked SIGTRAP's action, or Reverie could not tell SIGTRAP's state) stops the run at the hit with Hermit's refusal status, naming the thread, the watch and that state. Reverie claims a hit's SIGTRAP state only from a trap Linux reports as the hit's alone (TRAP_HWBKPT); a host whose debug status marks a hit as a single step too gets every hit reported Unknown, and --watch stops at its first hit there. Both test hosts report TRAP_HWBKPT. A thread that never arms is named in the trace, in the run summary's full report and on stderr.\n- Hits: GlobalTool::on_watchpoint_hit locks the scheduler and appends to the access trace through Scheduler::record_watch_hit: after = the events recorded so far, tid, slot, addr, ip and the stack's instruction pointers. A thread's first 64 hits are written as they happen; its last 64 after those, without stacks, at the end; the rest are counted.\n- Each thread reports its watch state with every event it records (a WatchStatus beside the access payload of TraceSchedEvent). At the event whose recording makes the watches due, the threads alive then (every thread with a next turn, and the one recording the event) enter the ledger, before any can exit unobserved, so one that stays parked until it is killed is still summarized. The trace ends with one watch_summary per such thread or thread seen later: armed_before (its first event recorded armed), unarmed (sigtrap_blocked, sigtrap_ignored, or no_stop for one that never reported again), hits and recorded. RunSummary gains watch {armed, unarmed, hits}, shown in the full report only, so the INFO view --verify compares is unchanged.\n- detcore-model access_trace: WatchHitRecord is now written; WatchSummaryRecord, WatchUnarmed, WATCH_HITS_WITH_STACKS and WATCH_HITS_KEPT_LAST are new.\n- racewrite_nostdlib gains a global, chosen, that both processes write after the fork (lines below line 66, so the analyze tests' line 35 is unchanged).\n\n## Determinism\n\nThe watch changes no schedule and no guest-visible result:\n\n1. No request is added. The order rides on replies the thread already receives, and the thread's state on requests it already sends; send_and_update_time charges a request from the sender's clock, not its size.\n2. Arming writes the stopped thread's debug registers through ptrace POKEUSER in the tracer. Nothing is injected into the guest, and RCBs count user mode only, so arming adds none.\n3. A hit is a forced SIGTRAP that Reverie suppresses, re-blocking a SIGTRAP the kernel unblocked; x86-64 data breakpoints are traps, reported after the access retires, so nothing runs twice. on_watchpoint_hit records no event, charges no time and only appends to the trace under the scheduler's lock. A hit that may have left the guest a SIGTRAP action it did not set stops the run instead.\n4. Where a hit's stop could still move a timer event (no precise-timer overflow records), Reverie says so per thread and --watch refuses the run instead.\n5. The scheduler's watch_order reads the recorded event count only; nothing in the scheduler reads the watch state back.\n\nThe equality tests below check this on the run's own schedule.\n\nPost-facto review: none of the four triggers applies. No syscall gains support (1). Detcore uses Reverie's watchpoint API, added in Reverie's own pull request, and changes no Tool, Guest or Backend API (2). Nothing is determinized differently: --watch only observes (3). And scheduling does not change (4): the with-and-without equality tests compare every recorded event, its thread and its virtual time.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nNo Linux behaviour Hermit presents changes. The design rests on:\n\n- Debug registers are per thread and not inherited: copy_thread zeroes the child's ptrace breakpoints (arch/x86/kernel/process.c:181 at v6.16), and exec drops them in flush_thread (flush_ptrace_hw_breakpoint, process.c:278), which is why every thread arms its own and starts over after exec.\n- A hit is sent as a forced SIGTRAP, which would unblock a blocked SIGTRAP and reset an ignored one (force_sig_info_to_task, kernel/signal.c:1294-1312 at v6.16); Reverie refuses to arm such a thread, and Detcore retries at the thread's next stop. When the guest blocks or ignores SIGTRAP after arming, Reverie re-blocks what it knows was blocked and reports a reset action or an unknown state, where --watch stops the run. https://elixir.bootlin.com/linux/v6.16/source/arch/x86/kernel/process.c\n\n## Validation\n\nStrict host: an Intel Xeon Gold 6138 bare-metal host, ptrace backend, PMU validated. Build host: an AMD EPYC VM. This branch builds on the commits below it, which point Reverie at its watchpoint change and carry its watchpoint count in the backend config.\n\n- Strict host, racewrite_nostdlib (built -no-pie) with --watch on its global chosen: a --strict --chaos --sched-seed=3 recording, two --strict replays of it at --log=info with the access trace, without and with the watch, and two fresh --strict --chaos --sched-seed=3 runs, without and with it. Each pair matches in exit status, stdout, stderr (empty), recorded schedule (replays 2,515 bytes, fresh 2,518), canonical INFO messages (48; 47) and every other trace record (9, an execve's launcher pointers blanked). Each watched run reports two hits on chosen, after event 5 by the forked child (tid 5) and after event 15 by the parent (tid 3), each with its stack, and both threads armed.\n- Strict host: --strict --chaos --verify --verify-strict --verify-json with --record-access-trace-to and --watch: bitwise_parity true, 47 INFO records compared, two hits.\n- cargo test -p hermit-run --test cli access_trace, on both hosts: 9 passed, among them watch_hits_name_both_writers_and_leave_the_run_unchanged (a replay and a fresh chaos run, each with and without the watch, equal in everything above, the launcher's execve arguments alone blanked; one hit from each writer), a_thread_that_blocks_sigtrap_never_arms_and_the_trace_says_so (watch_sigtrap_blocked.c: the worker's summary says sigtrap_blocked, no hits, and stderr names it), a_hit_that_resets_an_ignored_sigtrap_stops_the_run (watch_sigtrap_ignored.c: exit status 122, Hermit's refusal, and stderr names the thread and the SIGTRAP state Reverie reports, Unknown on the build host) and a_watch_survives_exec_into_an_image_that_stores_first (a static image whose first instructions store the watched global, exec'd into: one hit, by the thread that exec'd, after the exec). That last test also passes with rearm_after_exec removed: the post-exec stop records events whose replies already carry the order; the re-arm makes arming before the new image independent of that. The CLI tests run without --strict.\n- Hot word, strict host, release build: hotword N, one thread storing to a watched global in a loop, hermit run --strict with the access trace; medians of 5. 10,000 hits 0.41 s, 100,000 3.05 s, 1,000,000 32.6 s (about 30 µs a hit), against 0.04-0.05 s without the watch; with Reverie unwinding every hit (before WatchSpec::backtraces) 10,000 took 6.1 s and 100,000 44.9 s. The trace stays at 128 hit lines (64 with stacks) and 27.7 KB. In 12 interleaved rounds of unwatched and watched runs, every round's two runs record the same schedule; the one count that moved between rounds (291 against 267, before main) moved in both, when the host rewrote /etc/ld.so.cache, which the guest's loader maps and parses.\n- cargo test -p hermit-detcore --lib: the 15 that fail at the base on the build host fail, every other test passes. New: watch's watches_become_reverie_specs_and_a_new_thread_starts_unarmed and a_hit_that_may_have_changed_the_guest_stops_the_run (ActionReset and Unknown refused, naming the thread; Unchanged and Reblocked not), access_trace's watch_hits_are_capped_and_every_thread_is_summarized (the literal 64 and 64 bounds, the complete written sequence of 200 hits' first 64 and last 64, and a thread alive when the watches became due that never reports again, summarized as no_stop), scheduler's a_peer_alive_when_watches_become_due_is_summarized_if_killed_parked (at the threshold, and when the crossing event is the exit_group whose teardown kills the peer), and watch_arming_tests' a_thread_arms_when_due_and_retries_while_it_blocks_sigtrap (re-arming at exec) and a_watch_that_could_move_the_schedule_or_cannot_be_written_is_refused.\n- cargo test -p detcore-model --lib: 282 passed, among them watch::tests::a_watch_takes_an_address_a_length_and_an_access and config::tests::config_fingerprint_includes_the_watch_element_layout.\n- cargo nextest run -p hermit-run --lib --bin hermit: 937 run, 930 passed, 7 failed, all of them the host's, failing identically without this branch: 2 KVM tests (the VM's PMU counts outside KVM_RUN) and 5 ptrace_completion::tests::real_random_* tests (a child thread overflows its stack). New: run::watch_is_refused_where_it_could_not_arm_or_report (no trace, too many watches, a misaligned watch, --watch-from alone, a backend without watchpoints); run_config samples for --watch and --watch-from.\n- cargo fmt --all -- --check --verbose: 994 \"Formatting\" lines, no \"Diff in\". cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings: clean.\n- NEXTEST_EXPECTED_COUNTS, on this base's pins: regular_crates 1873 and regular_crates_on_host 1859 (+2 each), hermit_unit and hermit_unit_on_host 1108 (+1), detcore_unit and detcore_unit_on_host 2649 (+6), cli 623 and cli_on_host 542 (+4). cargo nextest list --profile ci finds exactly these for every cell but the regular_crates pair, whose full selection does not link outside the pinned root; its two new tests are both in detcore-model, which the on-host filter keeps. ci/sync-cell-config.sh regenerated validate.json and --check reports every file current; it ran with a CARGO_HOME whose config.toml carries the same Reverie [patch], since its rust-script steps do not read the workspace's.\n\n### Hot-word overhead\n\nStrict host, release build. Guest `hotword N`: one thread stores to a watched global `N` times in a loop (`volatile long hot; for (i = 0; i < n; i++) hot = i;`, built `-O1 -no-pie`). Each run is `hermit run --strict` with `--record-preemptions-to` and `--record-access-trace-to`, with and without `--watch` on `hot`; the times are medians of 5 runs.\n\n| N (hits) | without --watch | before: a stack at every hit | after: 64 stacks per watch and thread (`WatchSpec::backtraces`) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 0 | 0.04-0.05 s | 0.050 s | 0.039 s |\n| 10,000 | 0.04-0.05 s | 6.1 s (~610 µs a hit) | 0.41 s (~37 µs a hit) |\n| 100,000 | 0.04-0.05 s | 44.9 s (~450 µs a hit) | 3.05 s (~30 µs a hit) |\n| 1,000,000 | 0.04-0.05 s | not run (~7.5 min) | 32.6 s (~33 µs a hit) |\n\nBefore, Reverie unwound the stack with libunwind at every hit, and the 64-stack cap only re-armed the thread without stacks at its next Detcore stop, which a loop with no syscall and no preemption never reaches. Now each watch asks Reverie for the stacks of its first 64 hits on the thread, a budget Reverie spends at the hits, and Detcore has no re-arm path. The trace stays at 128 hit lines (64 with stacks, then the last 64 without) and 27.7 KB, with the summary counting all N hits.\n\nEquality: in 12 interleaved rounds of unwatched and watched runs (N=100,000, plain slot scheduling, one CPU set), every round's two runs record the same schedule, so the watch does not perturb the run. The one count that moved between rounds (the Branch run at event 105, inside the dynamic loader, 267 in rounds 1-3 and 291 in rounds 4-12) moved in both modes at once: the host rewrote `/etc/ld.so.cache` between rounds 3 and 4 (37,023 to 36,979 bytes, the size the loader maps), and Hermit does not freeze host files. Runs that read different host files are not expected to match, with or without the watch.\n\nBackend: ptrace. Log level: default, and info where stated. Relaxations: none on the strict host; the CLI tests run without --strict. Assurance: L2 on the strict host for racewrite_nostdlib with the watch on; the equality tests compare a whole recorded schedule. At head `01f999643`, on the AMD rebase host (performance counters fail validation): `cargo test -p hermit-run --features third-party-backends --test cli -- access_trace::` ran 9 tests, 9 passed, among them all four watch tests and main's an_access_trace_leaves_a_replayed_run_unchanged, after the last commit, which shares the branch-counter warning's prefix with the tests' stderr filter (the filter had main's old wording, so both tests failed on such hosts); `cargo nextest run -p hermit-detcore --lib` ran 3005, 3005 passed; `cargo clippy -p hermit-detcore -p hermit-run -p detcore-model --all-targets -- -D warnings` is clean. The --strict runs above are from an earlier revision on the bare-metal Intel host.\n\n## Not claimed\n\nWatchpoints on aarch64 or any backend but ptrace (refused); accesses the kernel makes for a syscall (Linux does not report them); two unwatched windows: a new glibc thread runs `start_thread`'s prologue with SIGTRAP still blocked, so it arms its watches only once it unblocks SIGTRAP, and a thread's code between its creation and its first stop with a reply runs before it arms; a thread that blocks SIGTRAP at every stop (reported, not watched); a run on, after a hit that changed the guest's SIGTRAP state (refused).\n\n\n## Review record\n\n- Codex-family (session ffa67a9e): CHANGES-REQUESTED at bada23035 (exec re-arm, NoStop summaries, fingerprint, test blind spots), 9566ad8fc (NoStop seeding one event late) and 4aef15ae0 (stale hosted pin); APPROVE at bee959e3c and at fea6656d0 (re-pointed at Reverie 7013ef867). The head a3b60eaa1 is fea6656d0 rebased onto #4739 ee2676261, with the sample-rows commit dropped (the base carries it); its code patches are identical (range-diff differs only in pins and pin comments).\n\nRevision (2026-10-27): rebased onto #4739's head 791ccdd65 (main 60d740b91). The DROP commit now points at Reverie 44669fc87 and its [patch] table covers 21 crates (reverie-linuxemu added). The `--watch` commit carries the 33-field legacy list now that main's `annotated_priority_replay` joins it, and a new commit records RPC revision 6 (the TraceSchedEvent retype and WithWatchOrder) in main's three-field row. Gates at this head: fmt and workspace clippy clean; detcore lib 2720/2720 under nextest; detcore-model 295; access_trace and watch CLI tests 9/9; derive-pins lists every cell equal to its pin. Known: `sync --check` fails only at its scorecard step, because that script builds detcore-model outside the root workspace where the DROP commit's [patch] does not reach, so it compiles against the pinned Reverie without `hardware_watchpoints`. Every other sync step passes; this goes away when the real Reverie pin bump replaces the DROP commit.\n\n**Revision 2:** rebased onto main `6f6291158`, on #4739's new head `78b948cb1`. Code conflicts resolved: its DROP BEFORE MERGE Reverie commit is re-stacked: local Reverie branch rb19/watch (445ce5f5b) is 44669fc8 picked onto main's pin 56bc0f896 (its parent be5b1264 is on main as 72f0077d), one keep-both conflict in reverie-ptrace/src/task.rs; the gitlink points at 445ce5f5b and Cargo.lock drops the Reverie source lines; a duplicated \"/// unset.\" doc line in config.rs is removed. Pins (main's value plus this change's tests, each matching `cargo nextest list` except the two regular_crates cells, below): `test.cli` 631 → 675, `test.cli_on_host` 550 → 594, `test.detcore_unit` and `_on_host` 2720 → 2826, `test.hermit_unit` 1124 → 1190, `test.hermit_unit_on_host` 1118 → 1184, `test.regular_crates` 1889 → 1908, `test.regular_crates_on_host` 1875 → 1894. Re-run at the new head (AMD host): `-p hermit-detcore --lib`: 2826 of 2826 passed; `-p hermit-run --lib` `watch_is_refused_where_it_could_not_arm_or_report`: 1 of 1 passed; `-p hermit-run --test cli` 4 named tests: 3 of 4 passed; the other one, `watch_hits_name_both_writers_and_leave_the_run_unchanged`, fails on this host only because the inexact-branch-counter warning both runs print carries host-measured SpecLockMap counts that differ between the runs (this AMD host's counter fails Reverie's validation); on a host whose counter passes there is no warning; `-p detcore-model --lib` `a_watch_takes_an_address_a_length_and_an_access`, `config_fingerprint_includes_the_watch_element_layout`: 2 of 2 passed. Its global RPC revision record for this change's GlobalRequest/GlobalResponse/Resources changes is regenerated as revision 9 in main's three-field row (enums and Resources digests at the new head): main now records revisions 1 to 7, and `the_global_rpc_revision_matches_its_definitions` fails until one is recorded. Two checks cannot run on this head, as at revision 1: its DROP BEFORE MERGE commit's path-form Reverie `[patch]` makes the workspace listing for `test.regular_crates` and its twin fail in hermit-install's build script with E0460 (`reverie_sabre` built against a second `reverie_inguest`), and `ci/sync-cell-config.sh`'s scorecard builds outside the workspace patch, so it does not see the patched Reverie and stops at the new field; fmt and `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings` are clean. Revision 1 was `4f4ef615c`.\n\n**Revision 3:** rebased onto main `7322689fa`, on #4739's new head `af74c39dd`. Code conflicts resolved: train 11's `detcore/src/tool_local.rs` changes conflicted with #4738's access-trace commit beneath this change (resolved there); Reverie #1161 landed (124b65b33, in main's pin 0afcb66a0), so the DROP BEFORE MERGE watch commit is gone and this change builds on main's pin, whose watchpoint API (`reverie/src/watchpoint.rs`, `tool.rs`, `capabilities.rs`) is byte-identical to the watch commit's; main's own fix-forward for that pin (71fbc1e06, with dbf8418c1's golden row) already reads `hardware_watchpoints` as 0 in the legacy form and widens the three config tests, so the backend-config commit keeps main's code and tests and carries only its count of 0 in `DEFAULT_BACKEND_CAPABILITIES` and its doc lines; and `RunSummary`'s new `watch` field follows main's aarch64 store-exclusive fields, with `WatchRunSummary` after main's `StoreExclusiveCounts` and `counted`. Pins (main's value plus this change's tests, each matching `cargo nextest list` apart from the inherited offset noted below): `test.cli` 675 → 712, `test.cli_on_host` 594 → 631, `test.detcore_unit` and `_on_host` 2826 → 2936, `test.hermit_unit` 1190 → 1224, `test.hermit_unit_on_host` 1184 → 1218, `test.regular_crates` 1908 → 1919, `test.regular_crates_on_host` 1894 → 1907. Re-run at the new head (AMD host): `-p hermit-detcore --lib`: 2929 of 2929 passed; `-p hermit-run --lib` `watch_is_refused_where_it_could_not_arm_or_report`: 1 of 1 passed; `-p hermit-run --test cli` 4 named tests: 3 of 4 passed; the other one stops at this host's `--strict` refusal (exit 122), so it gives no result here; `-p detcore-model --lib` `a_watch_takes_an_address_a_length_and_an_access`, `config_fingerprint_includes_the_watch_element_layout`: 2 of 2 passed. The count pins are main's values plus this change's tests; `test.detcore_unit` and `_on_host` stay 7, and `test.regular_crates_on_host` 2, above `cargo nextest list`, an offset inherited from the base 7322689fa (main recounted in 076e87dc2/02b8e145d), and rustfmt flags only the three pin-comment spacing hunks that base carries (fixed on main since). Revision 2 was `8d3d580bd`.\n\n**Revision 4:** rebased onto main `7e2d8ef1f`, on #4739's new head `d1bc26c40` (Reverie pin unchanged). Code conflicts resolved: #4738 landed as the lander's port ffe2ed4e1, so this change now sits on main 7e2d8ef1f through #4739 and replays only its own commits against the port. 9edbc28a9 summary.rs RunSummary: the watch field sits after main's guest_ids and host_id_tasks inside the struct (the automatic keep-both had put it after main's is_zero). 9edbc28a9 against #4738's port ffe2ed4e1: config.rs legacy form keeps the port's access_trace_max_bytes and main's detlog_anon and adds watch and watch_from after them (FIELDS_WITHOUT_A_LEGACY_KEY 46, forty-five fewer, so thirty-one keys fewer, assert keys.len() + 31), the round-trip spelling puts --watch after the port's --access-trace-max-bytes as Config's Display writes them, the run.rs rerun note names --access-trace-max-bytes too, and the pins are the port's main plus this commit's tests. Pins (main's value plus this change's tests, each matching `cargo nextest list`): `test.cli` 712 → 747, `test.cli_on_host` 631 → 666, `test.detcore_unit` and `_on_host` 2936 → 3000, `test.hermit_unit` 1224 → 1280, `test.hermit_unit_on_host` 1218 → 1274, `test.regular_crates` 1919 → 1929, `test.regular_crates_on_host` 1907 → 1915. `ci/sync-cell-config.sh --check`, fmt and `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings` are clean. Re-run at the new head (AMD host): `-p hermit-detcore --lib`: 3000 of 3000 passed; `-p hermit-run --lib` `watch_is_refused_where_it_could_not_arm_or_report`: 1 of 1 passed; `-p hermit-run --test cli` 4 named tests: 3 of 4 passed; the other one stops at this AMD rebase host's PMU check, which refuses `--strict` (exit 122), not evidence either way; `-p detcore-model --lib` `a_watch_takes_an_address_a_length_and_an_access`, `config_fingerprint_includes_the_watch_element_layout`: 2 of 2 passed. Revision 3 was `9643ed026`.\n\n**Revision 5:** rebased onto main `27c4c4017`, on #4739's new head `eebbb46b8` (Reverie pin unchanged). Code conflicts resolved: #4738 landed as the lander's port ffe2ed4e1, so this change now sits on main 7e2d8ef1f through #4739 and replays only its own commits against the port. 9edbc28a9 summary.rs RunSummary: the watch field sits after main's guest_ids and host_id_tasks inside the struct 